{
  "id": "ch1-main-line",
  "title": "The main line: 4...dxc4 5.a4 Bf5",
  "intro": "This is the chapter to know cold. After 4.Nc3 Black takes on c4 and then gets the bishop out to f5, and every other line in the course is judged against how comfortable this one is.\n\n## Why Black takes\n\nTaking on c4 is not a pawn grab. It clears the d5 square so the bishop on c8 can reach f5 with nothing hanging behind it, and it makes White spend a move recovering the pawn. Black gives the pawn back happily. What Black will not give back is the bishop's freedom.\n\n## The one long line\n\nAgainst 5.a4 Bf5 the main road runs 6.e3 e6 7.Bxc4 Bb4 8.O-O Nbd7 9.Qe2 Bg6 10.e4 O-O 11.Bd3 Bh5. It looks like a lot of moves, and it is one idea repeated: get every piece to a sensible square, keep the light-squared bishop, and let White have the space. White breaks with e4 and e5, Black meets it with ...Nd5 and takes back on d5 with the c-pawn, which opens the c-file.\n\n## The other two tries\n\n6.Ne5 wins the pawn back with the knight instead. Black answers 6...Nbd7 and gets a free ...e5 in a move later, which is the easiest equality in the whole course. 6.Nh4 spends two moves trading the bishop off; Black lets it happen, recaptures with the e-pawn and gets the e-file plus a pawn covering e4.\n\n## Without a4\n\n5.e3 and 5.e4 let Black play ...b5 and keep the pawn for a while. Both give Black a fine game, and both need Black to remember one thing: give the pawn back rather than tie the whole position to holding it.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nTrying to hang on to c4 after 5.a4 with ...b5, when axb5 and b3 blow the queenside open. Trading on c3 for no reason, which hands White the bishop pair and a centre. Grabbing on e4 in the main line, when the bishop has already gone to h5 and nothing takes back. And castling one move before ...Bg6, so that e4 arrives with a hit on the bishop and Black plays the same move a tempo down.",
  "defaultTags": ["development"],
